{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>That the loops correspond, one to the other.</b> Ensure that you make the loops in a single measure, their spacing matched this one to that one — and as their measure is in one sheet, so shall it be in its mate; so that when one joined set is spread out beside the other joined set, the loops of this sheet will be aligned opposite the loops of that one. And this is the sense of מַקְבִּילוֹת — this one opposite that one; it is the Targum's rendering of 'opposite' — לָקֳבֵל. The sheets: their length is 28 and their width is 4. When five sheets are joined together, their width comes to twenty; and so too the second joined set. And the Mishkan, its length is thirty from east to west, as it says: 'twenty boards for the south side' and likewise for the north; and each board is a cubit and a half — that makes thirty from east to west. The width of the Mishkan from north to south is ten cubits, as it says: 'and for the flank of the Mishkan westward…' and 'and two boards for the corners' — that makes ten; and I shall explain them in their place for these verses. You set the sheets with their length along the width of the Mishkan: the ten middle cubits for the roof over the interior width of the Mishkan; and one cubit this way and one cubit that way for the thickness of the tops of the boards, whose thickness is a cubit. That leaves sixteen cubits — eight for the north and eight for the south — covering the height of the boards, which are ten cubits high; so the bottom two cubits are exposed. The total width of the sheets is forty cubits when joined, twenty cubits per joined set. Thirty of them form the roof over the interior of the Mishkan along its length; one cubit covers the thickness of the board-tops on the west; and one cubit covers the thickness of the pillars on the east — for there were no boards on the east, but five pillars on which the curtain was spread and hung by hooks on them like a curtain. That leaves eight cubits hanging over the back of the Mishkan on the west, and the bottom two cubits exposed. This I found in the Baraita of Forty-Nine Measures. But in tractate Shabbat the sheets do not cover the eastern pillars, and nine cubits hang behind the Mishkan. And the verse in this passage supports us: 'and you shall place the curtain *beneath* the clasps' — and if according to this Baraita, the curtain would be drawn one cubit westward from the clasps."], :book :exodus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:54:44.099656178Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}